  story:[ "<b>The yard still smells of burned oil.</b> Relay targets remain latched, and the surviving tank wall carries a dark tide mark beneath the bushings.", "Operator Nkemi, The Oil Technician, and The Clerk hold the operating observations, laboratory samples, and asset decisions that shaped the unit's final months.", "You can blame <b>Sabotage or an attack on the yard</b> or accept <b>A freak lightning strike — an act of God</b>. Neither answer explains why ordinary records began to diverge.", "Temporary generation can hold the city for eight days. After that, pressure to energize a replacement may outrun the inquiry." ],
  endings:{ overclaimWhat:"attack", dismissalWhat:"freak",
    win:{ expertTitle:"The Transformer History Reassembled", expert:["You identify <b>Bram Odell — utility operator</b>, <b>The Utility's Asset Office</b>, and <b>Chronic overload with insulating-oil testing deferred</b>. Not sabotage or an attack on the yard. Not a freak lightning strike — an act of god. Load records, gas generation, oil condition, and relay operation describe progressive internal damage.","The finding distinguishes the final electrical fault from the months of thermal and dielectric aging that made the fire possible."], soundTitle:"The Asset Case Carries Load", sound:["You connect <b>Bram Odell — utility operator</b> with <b>The Utility's Asset Office</b> and prove <b>Chronic overload with insulating-oil testing deferred</b>. The condition evidence is credible, although the exact gas-to-fault interpretation is brief.","The inquiry accepts the result because operational loading and laboratory oil evidence converge on the same transformer deterioration."], namedTitle:"The Failed Asset Correctly Named", named:["You choose <b>Bram Odell — utility operator</b>, <b>The Utility's Asset Office</b>, and <b>Chronic overload with insulating-oil testing deferred</b>, but do not fully show how overload consumed insulation life before the final trip.","The accusation holds; the utility still needs a complete thermal-aging and maintenance chronology for fleet action."] },
    overclaim:{ title:"Attack Theory Overloads the Evidence", body:["You select <b>Sabotage or an attack on the yard</b>, yet no intrusion theory explains the long hot-running trend, overdue oil work, and gases formed inside the sealed tank.","The dramatic claim makes <b>Chronic overload with insulating-oil testing deferred</b> easier to dismiss, even though asset records support that narrower transformer finding."] },
    dismissal:{ title:"Lightning Used as an Asset Excuse", body:["You accept <b>A freak lightning strike — an act of God</b>, treating the final flash as cause enough while the transformer had already exceeded its condition margin.","That dismissal returns other overloaded units to service without the oil tests and loading discipline this failure demands."] },
    wrongNames:{ title:"Condition Found, Asset Trail Missed", body:["You identify <b>Chronic overload with insulating-oil testing deferred</b> but place the decisive responsibility or records elsewhere. The transformer chronology culminates with"] } },
